<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Renaud de Dammartin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1165–<a href="/years/1227">1227</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Renaud I, Count of Dammartin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Renaud de Dammartin was count of Dammartin and, through his marriage to Countess Ida in 1191, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>. Falling out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Emperor Otto IV and fought against Philip at Bouvines in 1214, where he was captured. He lost his lands and died in prison in 1227.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/renaud-de-dammartin.jpg" alt="Renaud de Dammartin" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prisonniers_Bouvines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Renaud, or Reginald, was the eldest son of Alberic III of Dammartin and Matilda of Clermont. Brought up at the French court, he was a childhood friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip Augustus</a>, but at his father's insistence he fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">Plantagenets</a>. Received back into Philip's favour, he married Marie of Châtillon, daughter of Guy II of Châtillon and Adèle of Dreux, a royal cousin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1191 his father kidnapped Ida, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, and had Renaud marry her, so that the county became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the French king rather than of the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. The marriage made him a power but also made him enemies in the Dreux family and in that of the count of Guînes, who had been betrothed to Ida. In 1203 he and Ida gave Boulogne a merchants' charter, probably for payment. Philip made him count of Aumale in 1204 and, after taking Normandy, granted him Mortain and the honour of Warenne, centred on Mortemer and Bellencombre.</p><p>Renaud nonetheless drew away from the king. In 1211 he refused to appear before Philip in a suit with Philippe de Dreux, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>, and Philip seized his lands. On 4 May 1212 at Lambeth he made an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in England and an annuity, and he drew other nobles, among them the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, into the coalition against Philip; each promised not to make a separate peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1214 he joined Emperor Otto IV and Count Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a>, where he commanded the Brabançons. He was one of the last to surrender and refused to submit to Philip. His lands went to the king's illegitimate son Philip Hurepel, who married Renaud's daughter Matilda II. Held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a> for the rest of his life, he was chained to a heavy log, as the historian Jim Bradbury describes, and lost hope when Ferdinand was released. His life ended in suicide.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reginald I of Dammartin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count <b>Renaud de Dammartin</b> fought against Philip II at Bouvines in 1214 and lost his lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-boulogne">County of Boulogne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Saint-Pierre-la-Garenne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.15,1.4&amp;year=1205" aria-label="Saint-Pierre-la-Garenne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4582 449 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Renaud de Dammartin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5032 719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5032 719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.15,1.4&amp;year=1205">What was here in 1205? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
